{{nihongo|The '''Imperial House of Okaiken'''|皇室|Kōshitsu}}, also known as the House of Honda or the Wisteria Throne, is the reigning dynasty of [[Okaiken]]. It consists of the members of the family of the reigning Emperor who perform official and public duties. Under the current governance model of the Forge State, the Emperor serves as the Head of State with significant reserve powers following the [[Jutsu Restoration of 2011]]. The dynasty is characterized by its focus on industrial mastery, technical precision, and the preservation of national heritage through the [[Jutsu era]].

== History and Restoration ==

The imperial house recognizes 126 monarchs in a continuous agnatic line. Historically, the reign of Emperor [[Masahito]] is divided into two phases. From 1985 to 2011, the monarchy operated under a symbolic framework known as the Silent Years. Following the Referendums of 2011, the Jutsu Restoration legally elevated the Emperor's status, granting the throne an active role in national governance and industrial oversight. This shift defined the modern ideology of the Forge State, where the monarchy acts as a check against parliamentary instability and a patron of heavy industry.

== Current members ==

The Emperor is the head of the imperial family and the sovereign of Okaiken.

[[Masahito]] (born 23 October 1967) is the reigning Emperor. His era name, Jutsu, reflects a national commitment to technique and industrial excellence. He is noted for his personal austerity and his daily practice of traditional archery.

[[Michiko]] (born 20 October 1970) is the Empress consort. Born into the Shoda industrial family, she is known as the Steel Matriarch for her bridge-building between the throne and the manufacturing sector. She oversees social welfare initiatives for industrial laborers.

[[Crown Prince Tomohito]] (born 2002), known as the Iron Prince, is the heir apparent. A graduate in physical sciences and a former rugby player, he represents the structural integrity and raw power of the state. He spent his high school years incognito as Tomo Honda to experience the life of a commoner.

[[Princess Kiko]] (born 2006) is the middle child. A landscape architecture student, she manages the Komorebi Protocol, a system focused on environmental remediation and balancing industrial output with ecological sustainability. She is often described as the soft conscience of the family.

[[Prince Teruhito]] (born 2010), known as the Jade Prince, is the youngest child. A student and classical musician, he represents the digital and empathetic soul of the monarchy. He is associated with the Letta AI project, which aims to harmonize technology with human-centric values.

== Role in the Forge State ==

The Imperial House operates as a functional engine for national stability. The family members are educated in rigorous technical and physical disciplines, a concept known as Functional Royalty. 

The Emperor holds weekly audiences with the Prime Minister and exercises oversight on infrastructure and defense spending. The Crown Prince is a patron of metallurgy and shipbuilding, while the Empress manages the Steel Families Initiative to support the children of factory workers. This involvement ensures that the monarchy remains a practical participant in the nation's industrial success.

== Residences and estates ==

The primary residence of the Imperial Family is the Imperial Palace in Okami. The estate includes the Togu Palace and various industrial testing grounds used for royal patronage events. Other significant properties include the Mito Imperial Palace and private villas in Tokuyama and [[Toyozawa]]. Unlike many contemporary monarchies, the Okaiken Imperial House maintains active ties to industrial estates and maritime shipyards as part of their official duties.

